<header><p class="st-kicker">other · South Asia</p><h1 class="st-title">Bombay Presidency</h1><p class="st-meta">1668 CE – 1947 CE · capital Bombay</p></header>
<p class="st-teaser">The British province of western India governed from Bombay, which grew from a set of marshy islands into India's commercial capital.</p>
<div class="st-body"><div class="st-text"><h2>Origins</h2><p>Portugal ceded <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mumbai">Bombay</a> to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/england">England</a> in 1661 as part of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/catherine-of-braganza">Catherine of Braganza's</a> dowry, and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/charles-ii-of-england">Charles II</a> leased it to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/east-india-company">East India Company</a> in 1668. The Company's western presidency moved from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/surat">Surat</a> to Bombay in 1687. The presidency expanded greatly after the defeat of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/maratha-confederacy">Maratha Confederacy</a> in 1818 and the conquest of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sindh">Sindh</a> in 1843.</p><h2>Rule and society</h2><p>A governor in council ruled from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mumbai">Bombay</a>, under the Governor-General from 1773. Land reclamation joined the islands, and Bombay became a hub of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cotton">cotton</a> trade, especially during the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/american-civil-war">American Civil War</a>, and of textile mills, shipping and banking dominated by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/parsis">Parsi</a>, Gujarati and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/baghdadi-jews">Baghdadi Jewish</a> merchants. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mountstuart-elphinstone">Mountstuart Elphinstone</a> organised the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/deccan">Deccan</a> conquests. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sindh">Sindh</a> was administered as part of the presidency until 1936.</p><h2>Decline and legacy</h2><p><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mumbai">Bombay</a> became a province of the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/empire/british-raj">British Raj</a>, and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/indian-national-congress">Indian National Congress</a> was founded in the city in 1885. In 1947 Bombay Province joined the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/dominion-of-india">Dominion of India</a>; in 1960 it was divided into <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/maharashtra">Maharashtra</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gujarat">Gujarat</a>.</p></div><aside class="st-side"><figure class="st-fig"><img src="/images/states/bombay-presidency.jpg" alt="Bombay Presidency" width="320" height="160"><figcaption>Image: Motiv888 &amp; Cypp0847 (original), Sbb1413 (derived work) · CC BY-SA 4.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Blue_Ensign_of_British_Bombay.svg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">source</a></figcaption></figure><dl class="st-facts"><dt>Years</dt><dd>1668 CE – 1947 CE</dd><dt>Centuries</dt><dd><a href="/centuries/17th-century">17th century</a> – <a href="/centuries/20th-century">20th century</a></dd><dt>Type</dt><dd>other</dd><dt>Character</dt><dd>colonial</dd><dt>Capital</dt><dd>Bombay</dd><dt>Rulers</dt><dd>Gerald Aungier, Mountstuart Elphinstone</dd><dt>Religion</dt><dd>Hinduism, Islam, Zoroastrianism</dd><dt>Language</dt><dd>Marathi, Gujarati, English</dd><dt>Today</dt><dd><a href="/countries/india">India</a>, <a href="/countries/pakistan">Pakistan</a></dd><dt>Also</dt><dd>Presidency of Bombay, Bombay Province</dd></dl><figure class="st-locator"><a href="/atlas?year=1808&amp;focus=bombay-presidency" aria-label="Bombay Presidency on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="6650 1475 640 512" role="img" aria-label="Map: where Bombay Presidency lay"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-tie" d="M6970 1731L4997 651M6970 1731L7364 1601M6970 1731L7364 1602"/><path class="map-dot-n" d="M4997 651h0M7364 1601h0M7364 1602h0"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M6970 1731h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M6970 1731h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks its recorded location. <a h